<p><img src="https://cdn3.brettterpstra.com/uploads/2026/10/themable-nvalt-header-rb.7539.jpg" width="800" height="226" style="margin:0 auto;clear:both;" class="header" /></p>
<p>nvALT can be dressed up with themes now. The editor takes a MultiMarkdown Composer-compatible theme, the preview takes a Marked stylesheet (which is just CSS, any will work), and the notes list and window colors can be styled, too.</p>


              
              <p><img src="https://cdn3.brettterpstra.com/uploads/2026/10/nvalt-style-2-800.jpg"></p>
              
              <figcaption>nvALT with Svelte theme</figcaption>
            

<p>I&rsquo;m just having fun with this. For truly customizable theming, you&rsquo;re going to want <a href="https://multimarkdown.com/" title="MultiMarkdown Composer">MultiMarkdown Composer</a> or <a href="https://nvultra.com/" title="nvUltra-Searchable, portable, MultiMarkdown notes">nvUltra</a>. I&rsquo;m not trying to steal thunder from either app (even though I am, a little).</p>

<h3 id="pick-one-in-settings">Pick one in Settings</h3>

<p>Open <strong>Settings → Fonts &amp; Colors</strong>. There are two menus now, one for the editor and one for the preview, plus <strong>Import…</strong> and <strong>Open Themes Folder</strong>.</p>

<p><strong>Default</strong> is the nvALT you already have: the color scheme you set in that same pane, and the preview from <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">template.html</code> / <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">custom.css</code>. Choose a named theme when you want the file to take over.</p>

<p>Everything lives in one folder:</p>

<div class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight fixed"><code>~/Library/Application Support/nvALT/Themes</code></pre></div></div>

<p>Drop a file in there, or use <strong>Import…</strong> and pick a few. <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">.theme</code>, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">.plist</code>, and <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">.json</code> show up as editor themes. <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">.css</code> shows up as a preview theme. The first time you open themes, a sample pair called <strong>Paper</strong> is copied in. It is not selected for you.</p>

<h3 id="composer-themes-as-is">Composer themes, as-is</h3>

<p>An editor theme is a <a href="https://multimarkdown.com/">MultiMarkdown Composer</a>-compatible theme. The <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">.theme</code> files Composer writes are JSON, with the same keys you would see in a property list. nvALT also reads a real XML or binary plist, and a <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">.json</code> file that uses those keys. Export a theme from Composer, or copy one out of its themes folder, and import it. No conversion step.</p>

<p>Svelte, one of my own and my personal favorite, is a good one to steal as an example. The window and the notes list sit on a blue-gray, and the note itself is a little darker:</p>

<div class="language-json highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight fixed"><code><span class="nl">"backgroundColor"</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">:</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s2">"#434c5f"</span><span class="err">,</span><span class="w">
</span><span class="nl">"foregroundColor"</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">:</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s2">"#e5e9f0"</span><span class="err">,</span><span class="w">

</span><span class="nl">"tableViewHighlighted"</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">:</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w">
    </span><span class="nl">"backgroundColor"</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">:</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s2">"#8ebcbb"</span><span class="w">
</span><span class="p">}</span><span class="err">,</span><span class="w">

</span><span class="nl">"textView"</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">:</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w">
    </span><span class="nl">"backgroundColor"</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">:</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s2">"#3b4253"</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w">
    </span><span class="nl">"foregroundColor"</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">:</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s2">"#e5e9f0"</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w">
    </span><span class="nl">"caretColor"</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">:</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s2">"#86c0d1"</span><span class="w">
</span><span class="p">}</span><span class="w">
</span></code></pre></div></div>

<p><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">textView</code> paints the note: background, text, caret, selection. The notes list uses <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">tableView</code>, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">tableViewHighlighted</code>, and <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">tableViewInactive</code> when those exist, and falls back to the window colors when they do not. Syntax is a dictionary per element. <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">heading</code>, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">strong</code>, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">emphasis</code>, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">codeBlock</code>, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">codeSpan</code>, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">blockquote</code>, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">markup</code>, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">linkParens</code>, and the rest of the Composer set are all fair game. A heading level can sit on top of the base <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">heading</code> style.</p>

<p>Each style can set <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">foregroundColor</code>, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">backgroundColor</code>, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">markupForegroundColor</code>, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">bold</code>, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">italic</code>, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">strikethrough</code>, and <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">mono</code>. Colors are <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">#RGB</code>, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">#RRGGBB</code>, or <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">#RRGGBBAA</code> when you want the selection or a highlight to be translucent. <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">fontScale</code> is in the Composer format and changes the type size in Composer and nvUltra. nvALT currently ignores it, so the editor stays at the size you set in Fonts &amp; Colors.</p>

<p>Those colors stay on screen. They are not written back into the note, so switching to <strong>Default</strong> (or to another theme) does not bake the last palette into the file.</p>

<h3 id="editing-a-theme">Editing a theme</h3>

<p>Open the <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">.theme</code> file in any text editor. It is plain JSON. Change a hex color, save, and nvALT picks it up from the themes folder.</p>

<div class="language-json highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight fixed"><code><span class="nl">"heading1"</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">:</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w">
    </span><span class="nl">"foregroundColor"</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">:</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s2">"#eccc87"</span><span class="w">
</span><span class="p">}</span><span class="w">
</span></code></pre></div></div>

<p>The whole Svelte theme is in the repo: <a href="https://github.com/ttscoff/nv/blob/master/examples/Svelte.theme">Svelte.theme</a>. This link installs it:</p>

<p><a href="https://brettterpstra.comnvalt://add-theme?url=https%3A%2F%2Fraw.githubusercontent.com%2Fttscoff%2Fnv%2Fmaster%2Fexamples%2FSvelte.theme">Install Svelte</a></p>

<h3 id="marked-stylesheets-for-the-preview">Marked stylesheets for the preview</h3>

<p>Preview themes are just CSS. When one is selected, the note is rendered inside <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">&lt;div id="wrapper"&gt;</code>, which is the same hook Marked uses. A Marked theme can be dropped in and used directly. Style <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">#wrapper</code>, or style <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">h1</code>, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">p</code>, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">pre</code>, and the rest of the elements inside it. Rules on <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">body</code> still paint the page around the note.</p>

<p>A preview theme replaces <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">template.html</code> and <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">custom.css</code> for that preview. Those files are still there for <strong>Default</strong>, and they are still the right place for a custom template with your own JavaScript. A theme is the simpler path: one stylesheet, no template.</p>

<p>Here is the shape of a small preview theme:</p>

<div class="language-css highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight fixed"><code><span class="nt">body</span> <span class="p">{</span>
    <span class="nl">margin</span><span class="p">:</span> <span class="m">0</span><span class="p">;</span>
    <span class="nl">background</span><span class="p">:</span> <span class="nx">#f4f1ea</span><span class="p">;</span>
<span class="p">}</span>

<span class="nf">#wrapper</span> <span class="p">{</span>
    <span class="nl">max-width</span><span class="p">:</span> <span class="m">42em</span><span class="p">;</span>
    <span class="nl">margin</span><span class="p">:</span> <span class="m">0</span> <span class="nb">auto</span><span class="p">;</span>
    <span class="nl">padding</span><span class="p">:</span> <span class="m">2.4em</span> <span class="m">2.2em</span> <span class="m">4em</span><span class="p">;</span>
    <span class="nl">font</span><span class="p">:</span> <span class="m">18px</span><span class="o">/</span><span class="m">1.55</span> <span class="n">Palatino</span><span class="p">,</span> <span class="n">Georgia</span><span class="p">,</span> <span class="nb">serif</span><span class="p">;</span>
<span class="p">}</span>

<span class="nf">#wrapper</span> <span class="nt">h1</span> <span class="p">{</span>
    <span class="nl">font-family</span><span class="p">:</span> <span class="s1">"Avenir Next"</span><span class="p">,</span> <span class="n">Helvetica</span><span class="p">,</span> <span class="nb">sans-serif</span><span class="p">;</span>
<span class="p">}</span></code></pre></div></div>

<p>Two ready-made ones:</p>

<ul>
  <li><a href="https://github.com/ttscoff/nv/blob/master/examples/GitHub.css">GitHub.css</a> is the GitHub-style sheet Fletcher and I use. <a href="https://brettterpstra.comnvalt://add-theme?url=https%3A%2F%2Fraw.githubusercontent.com%2Fttscoff%2Fnv%2Fmaster%2Fexamples%2FGitHub.css">Install it</a>.</li>
  <li><a href="https://github.com/ttscoff/nv/blob/master/examples/Resolute.css">Resolute.css</a> is heavier. The fonts are embedded in the file, so it is a large download and a very finished page. <a href="https://brettterpstra.comnvalt://add-theme?url=https%3A%2F%2Fraw.githubusercontent.com%2Fttscoff%2Fnv%2Fmaster%2Fexamples%2FResolute.css">Install it</a>.</li>
</ul>

<p>Raw copies, if you would rather save the file yourself:</p>

<div class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight fixed"><code>https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ttscoff/nv/master/examples/Svelte.theme
https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ttscoff/nv/master/examples/GitHub.css
https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ttscoff/nv/master/examples/Resolute.css</code></pre></div></div>

<h3 id="install-from-a-link">Install from a link</h3>

<p><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">nvalt://</code> and <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">nv://</code> both accept a theme. The host is <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">add-theme</code> (or <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">import-theme</code>). Percent-encode the <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">url</code> value. A local file works the same way:</p>

<div class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight fixed"><code>nvalt://add-theme?url=https%3A%2F%2Fraw.githubusercontent.com%2Fttscoff%2Fnv%2Fmaster%2Fexamples%2FSvelte.theme
nv://add-theme?path=/Users/me/Desktop/GitHub.css</code></pre></div></div>

<p>The file is copied into the themes folder and selected. <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">url</code> can be <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">file</code>, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">http</code>, or <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">https</code>. <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">path</code> is a filesystem path, and a <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">~</code> in it is expanded.</p>

